# Generated data files keep LF on checkout, on every platform.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: core.autocrlf is true on the project machine, so git
# rewrites LF to CRLF when it checks a text file out. build_find_data.py's
# --check compares the bytes it would write against the bytes on disk, which is
# how a STALE generated file is detected (H6). With autocrlf converting on
# checkout, a freshly cloned repo would fail that comparison on line endings
# alone - a stale-detector reporting stale for a file that is perfectly current.
#
# A check that fires on something it was not built to catch is as useless as one
# that never fires: people learn to ignore it, and then it is not a check.
#
# So the generated files are pinned to LF end to end. They are machine-written
# and machine-read; nobody hand-edits them, so there is nothing to gain from a
# platform-native ending.
*.gen.js text eol=lf
